// en is CMUdict-derived, so wikipron eng_us (independent human Wiktionary transcriptions) is a genuine
// referee. It is noisy (proper nouns, British variants, letter-name entries) → a modest floor. We fold our
// narrow allophonic detail (aspiration, dark-l, r-coloured schwa) and our superscript diphthong offglides.
{
    "referees": [
        {
            "file": "en.wikipron-eng-latn-us-broad.tsv",
            "source": "wikipron eng_latn_us broad (human, GenAm)",
            "role": "primary",
            // ⚠ 10.7% OF A FILE LABELLED GenAm IS RP, and these rows are not noisy US transcriptions —
            // they are UK transcriptions in the wrong file. Validated against en-gb.wikipron-uk.tsv:
            // 92% of the RP-vowel rows and 98% of the non-rhotic rows appear there with a BYTE-IDENTICAL
            // reading. Scoring a rhotic GenAm engine against them marks it wrong for being right.
            // ⚠ NOT MOVED, because there is almost nothing to move: 471 of the 488 are already in the en-GB
            // referee. The other 17 (0.37% of the corpus — `vampire`, `Syriac`, `hydrochloric`,
            // `coefficient`…) are genuinely lost coverage, and that is the right trade: an RP reading scored
            // against a rhotic engine is worse than no reading, because it is wrong in a direction the
            // engine cannot fix. And the
            // source file is left INTACT — it is a provenance-tracked CC-BY-SA import, so the exclusion
            // lives here where it is reviewable and is reported on every run.
            "excludeRows": [
                {
                    "ipa": "əʊ|ɒ|ɪə|ʊə|ɛə",
                    "note": "RP-only vowels: GOAT əʊ (US oʊ), LOT ɒ (US ɑ), and the centring diphthongs ɪə/ʊə/ɛə, which a rhotic accent spells ɪɹ/ʊɹ/ɛɹ. 390 rows. ⚠ ɑː IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT and was in the first draft of this list: the backbone strips ː, so ɑː folds to ɑ — the US vowel — and 108 rows would have been dropped for a length mark that never reaches the comparison."
                },
                {
                    "spelling": "[aeiouyAEIOUY]r+(?![aeiouyAEIOUY])",
                    "ipaLacks": "[ɹɚɝɻr]",
                    "note": "NON-RHOTIC: spelled with a post-vocalic r, transcribed without any rhotic at all — Dunkirk dʌŋkɜːk, Hartburn hɑːtbən, Gentner ɡɛntnə, ticker tɪkə. 98 further rows. Needs BOTH fields: neither half says it alone. ⚠ ONE KNOWN FALSE POSITIVE in the 98, checked by reading all of them: `dossier` = dɑsieɪ, where the final -r really is silent in GenAm because the word is a French loan. Left in rather than special-cased — a one-word exception to a rule that is otherwise 97/98 clean costs more in machinery than the row is worth, and it is recorded here so the next reader does not rediscover it as a bug."
                },
                {
                    "spelling": "[aeiouy]r(e|ed)?$",
                    "ipaLacks": "[ɹɚɝɻr].{0,2}$",
                    "note": "NON-RHOTIC, WORD-FINAL: spelled with a word-final r (a silent `e` or an `-ed` allowed after it) and transcribed with NO rhotic in its last three symbols — `crowner` kɹaʊnə, `featured` fiːt͡ʃəd, `Dromore` dɹəmɔː, `tenderizer` tɛndəɹaɪzə. ⚠ THIS EXISTS BECAUSE THE RULE ABOVE SCANS THE WHOLE STRING and an ONSET r masks a non-rhotic coda: `kɹaʊnə` contains a `ɹ`, so `ipaLacks` was satisfied and the row survived. Found by sampling ten undetermined divergences and diagnosing each — it was one of the ten. 18 further rows, 17 of which the en-GB referee carries with a byte-identical reading. ⚠ `-s`/`-es` IS DELIBERATELY NOT ALLOWED after the r: there the r is usually the ONSET of the next syllable and is pronounced, so `Pescadores` pɛskədɔːɹiːz — correct GenAm — would have been dropped. Measured: allowing it adds one row and one false positive."
                },
                {
                    "spelling": "^.$",
                    "note": "A ONE-CHARACTER HEADWORD IS A GLYPH, NOT A WORD, and this referee has no fixed semantics for one: `m` ɛm, `p` piː, `q` kjuː and `a` eɪ are the letter's NAME, while `x` ks is the letter's SOUND. A row whose meaning is not constant within the file cannot arbitrate a reading. 6 rows. ⚠ IT IS SCORE-NEUTRAL BY CONSTRUCTION and that is the point — 3 of the 6 were already passing (m, p, q read as letter names) and 3 were not, so this moves `folded` by 0.01pp. What it removes is three PERMANENT divergences with no fix: `x` we read `ɛks` and misaki gold agrees, so the referee is simply wrong; `A`/`a` we read as the reduced article `ə`, DELIBERATELY, because a bare capital `A` in real text is overwhelmingly sentence-initial and a determiner. Not expressible via `intentional`, which is positionwise and single-character."
                }
            ]
        }
    ],
    "segmentJoin": true,
    "secondaryGap": "epitran eng-Latn is itself CMU-derived — circular with our CMUdict-based g2p, so not independent.",
    // ⚠ BEFORE THE BACKBONE, because the backbone strips combining marks and the syllabic one has to be
    // turned into a vowel first, not deleted. Without this `Anglican` folds to `æŋɡləkn` against the
    // referee's `æŋɡlɪkən` and every syllabic consonant is scored ALWAYS-WRONG — which means #1319, the PR
    // that ADDED the reduced slot misaki writes and Kokoro was trained on, LOWERED this score by being
    // right. Measured at 7.5% of in-dictionary disagreements. Same rule as KokoroFormat's SyllabicRe.
    //
    // ⚠ IT IS BIDIRECTIONAL, not a concession: the referee writes the mark too (60 of 4,558 US rows, 1,196
    // of 76,284 UK rows), so this normalises BOTH sides — their `zm̩` and our `zəm` meet at `zəm`, and so do
    // their `zəm` and our `zm̩`. Under the bare backbone strip the first pair met at `zm` vs `zəm` and missed.
    //
    // ⚠ AND IT IS NOT A FLEET-WIDE FIX — that was measured, because 43 referee files carry this mark and the
    // obvious conclusion is that BACKBONE should do this for everyone. It makes them WORSE:
    //     sr 26,048 → 25,529     cs 17,254 → 17,144     mk 62,375 → 62,314     de 3,732 → 3,711
    // In those languages BOTH sides write `r̩`/`n̩`, so deleting the mark already aligns them and turning it
    // into a vowel exposes a mere presence/absence difference instead. English is the reverse case: its
    // referee spells the same syllable with a real vowel. The right fold is language-specific and this is
    // English's.
    "preFolds": [
        { "pattern": "(\\S)\u0329", "replace": "ə$1", "note": "our syllabic sonorant n̩/l̩/m̩ vs the referee's ən/əl/əm — the referee's inventory has no syllabicity mark, so leaving it unfolded maps a documented convention to always-wrong (the #1282 principle, applied to the mark instead of to ᵻ)" }
    ],
    "folds": [
        {
            "pattern": "ᵻ",
            "replace": "ɪ",
            "note": "the weak vowel ᵻ has NO counterpart in the referee's inventory, so leaving it unfolded maps it to ALWAYS-WRONG — a uniform penalty on a documented house convention across 5,580 lexicon rows. Measured per slot against this referee: our ᵻ draws ɪ 72.9% / ə 19.5%, our full ɪ draws ɪ 91.2% / ə 4.2%, our ə draws ɪ 9.0% / ə 82.5% (118 ᵻ slots over 1,929 aligned words) — so ɪ is the best-supported single mapping by a wide margin. ⚠ IT IS NOT FREE: the referee PARTIALLY resolves ᵻ (our ᵻ and our full ɪ draw different ɪ-rates, 72% vs 93%), so this fold gives up catching an ᵻ written where a full ɪ belongs. That axis is kept visible outside the headline by tools/english/en_weak_vowel_survey.mts — see #1282."
        },
        {
            "pattern": "ʌ",
            "replace": "ə",
            "note": "STRUT and schwa are ONE PHONEME in English, in complementary distribution by stress — and this engine derives them that way: english.jsonc maps AH to ʌ when stressed and ə when not, from the same ARPABET symbol. The eval's backbone already strips stress, so leaving the pair unfolded smuggles a STRESS claim back into a comparison that is meant to be segmental. 42 rows differ by nothing else, 37 of them the referee writing ʌ where we write ə (`bugsona` bʌɡsoʊnə, `decussation` dɛkʌseɪʃən). ⚠ IT HIDES NOTHING CONTRASTIVE: English has no ʌ/ə minimal pair independent of stress, so unlike the cot–caught fold tried and rejected beside it, nothing lexical is lost."
        },
        {
            "pattern": "ʲ",
            "replace": "",
            "note": "our palatal on-glide before a front vowel (`Albania` ælbeɪniʲə) — narrow allophonic detail with no counterpart in the referee's broad inventory, exactly like the ʰ and ɫ folds beside it. 6.9% of in-dictionary disagreements."
        },
        {
            "pattern": "ɝ",
            "replace": "əɹ",
            "note": "STRESSED NURSE. ɚ is already folded to əɹ and ɝ was not, so the pair differed only by a stress the segmental backbone has already stripped — `advert` ours ædvəɹt vs referee ædvɝt. Folding both to the same sequence is what makes the ɚ/ɝ distinction invisible on BOTH sides rather than on one."
        },
        {
            "pattern": "eᶦ",
            "replace": "eɪ",
            "note": "our superscript FACE offglide eᶦ vs referee eɪ"
        },
        {
            "pattern": "oᶷ",
            "replace": "oʊ",
            "note": "our GOAT offglide oᶷ vs referee oʊ"
        },
        {
            "pattern": "aᶦ",
            "replace": "aɪ",
            "note": "our PRICE offglide aᶦ vs referee aɪ"
        },
        {
            "pattern": "aᶷ",
            "replace": "aʊ",
            "note": "our MOUTH offglide aᶷ vs referee aʊ"
        },
        {
            "pattern": "ɔᶦ",
            "replace": "ɔɪ",
            "note": "our CHOICE offglide ɔᶦ vs referee ɔɪ"
        },
        {
            "pattern": "ɚ",
            "replace": "əɹ",
            "note": "r-coloured schwa ɚ vs referee ə + ɹ (the letter r written out)"
        },
        {
            "pattern": "ʰ",
            "replace": "",
            "note": "allophonic aspiration on voiceless stops — referee omits it"
        },
        {
            "pattern": "ɫ",
            "replace": "l",
            "note": "dark-l is allophonic — referee writes plain l"
        }
    ],
    // ⚠ DECLARED-INTENTIONAL DIVERGENCES — credited as agreement, and NOT folds. A fold rewrites both
    // sides and so claims the two notations mean the same thing; these rewrite the REFEREE's string only,
    // because the class is DIRECTIONAL. Measured in
    // docs/investigations/en/en_suffix_weak_vowel_investigation.md Run 1, at exactly the disputed sites:
    //
    //     gold `ə` / ours `ɪ`   6,079 sites   UK referee 82.3% US   US referee 72.3% US   ← we are right
    //     gold `ɪ` / ours `ə`   1,462 sites   UK referee 87.0% ref  US referee 82.4% ref  ← we are WRONG
    //
    // Both directions look like one class and are not, which is why a `ə`↔`ɪ` fold would be wrong: it
    // would credit the second as well as the first. Only the first is declared here.
    //
    // ⚠ WHAT IS NOT HERE MATTERS AS MUCH. The cot–caught pair `ɔ`/`ɑ` was tested for this and REFUSED —
    // the referee writes `ɔ` in 312 of its 4,558 rows, so it records the distinction and merely assigns
    // ~38 words differently. That is a lexical disagreement where neither side has been shown right, and
    // crediting it would be marking our own errors correct.
    "intentional": [
        {
            "refHas": "ə",
            "weHave": "ɪ",
            "note": "the WEAK VOWEL, where the referee reduces and we do not. misaki and this referee write unstressed /ɪ/ as `ə`; we write `ɪ`, and both independent referees back us at the disputed sites (82.3% en-GB over 1,484 sites, 72.3% en-US over 95)."
        },
        {
            "refHas": "ɪ",
            "weHave": "ə",
            "note": "the SAME convention, the other way: the referee keeps /ɪ/ where CMUdict writes AH0 and we therefore write `ə`. ⚠ THIS DIRECTION WAS REFUSED ONCE AND THEN MEASURED. It looks like a defect class, and for one environment it is — so the environments where we have evidence CMUdict is wrong are OVERRIDDEN IN THE ENGINE rather than declared here (`-ist`, `-is`, `-age` in rebaseSuffixIh; `-est`, `-ed`, `-es`, `-ity`, `-ible` in isBarredI). What is left is the convention itself: at an unstressed AH0 spelled ⟨i⟩ — the shape of every remaining row — the en-GB referee writes `ɪ` 83% of the time and misaki's gold writes `ə` 88%, BOTH INTERNALLY CONSISTENT AND FLATLY OPPOSED over 2,059 slots. That is a notation choice, not an error, and we follow the one Kokoro was trained on. Arbitrating the rows individually, after `-est` took one of them: 134 rows = 54 where gold agrees with us, 5 where gold agrees with the referee, 75 no verdict. ⚠ SO THIS OVER-CREDITS BY FIVE ROWS (`Alice`, `creamily`, `inevitable`, `instil`, `minim`): lexical exceptions with no environment to key on. Recorded rather than hidden; if that set grows an environment, it becomes an engine rule and leaves here."
        }
    ]
}
